{"surah": 109, "verses": [{"ayah": 1, "text": "قُلْ يَٰٓأَيُّهَا ٱلْكَٰفِرُونَ ", "translation": "Say, \"O disbelievers,", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "قُلْ", "translit": "ْlُq", "root": "قول", "latinTranslit": "qul"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "يَٰٓأَيُّهَا", "translit": "āَhُّyَaʾَٰٓy", "root": "ايي", "latinTranslit": "yāayyuhā"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلْكَٰفِرُونَ", "translit": "َnwُrِfَٰkْlٱ", "root": "كفر", "latinTranslit": "l-kāfirūna"}], "hasIhya": true, "haleemTranslation": "Say [Prophet], ‘Disbelievers", "clearyTranslation": "Say [Prophet], ‘Disbelievers"}, {"ayah": 2, "text": "لَآ أَعْبُدُ مَا تَعْبُدُونَ ", "translation": "I do not worship what you worship.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "لَآ", "translit": "ٓāَl", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "lā"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "أَعْبُدُ", "translit": "ُdُbْعَaʾ", "root": "عبد", "latinTranslit": "aʿbudu"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "مَا", "translit": "āَm", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "mā"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "تَعْبُدُونَ", "translit": "َnwُdُbْعَt", "root": "عبد", "latinTranslit": "taʿbudūna"}], "hasIhya": false, "haleemTranslation": "I do not worship what you worship", "clearyTranslation": "I do not worship what you worship"}, {"ayah": 3, "text": "وَلَآ أَنتُمْ عَٰبِدُونَ مَآ أَعْبُدُ ", "translation": "Nor are you worshippers of what I worship.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَلَآ", "translit": "ٓāَlَw", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "walā"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "أَنتُمْ", "translit": "ْmُtnَaʾ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "antum"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "عَٰبِدُونَ", "translit": "َnwُdِbَٰع", "root": "عبد", "latinTranslit": "ʿābidūna"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "مَآ", "translit": "ٓāَm", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "mā"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "أَعْبُدُ", "translit": "ُdُbْعَaʾ", "root": "عبد", "latinTranslit": "aʿbudu"}], "hasIhya": false, "haleemTranslation": "you do not worship what I worship", "clearyTranslation": "you do not worship what I worship"}, {"ayah": 4, "text": "وَلَآ أَنَا۠ عَابِدٌ مَّا عَبَدتُّمْ ", "translation": "Nor will I be a worshipper of what you worship.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَلَآ", "translit": "ٓāَlَw", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "walā"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "أَنَا۠", "translit": "۠āَnَaʾ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "anā"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "عَابِدٌ", "translit": "ٌdِbāَع", "root": "عبد", "latinTranslit": "ʿābidun"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "مَّا", "translit": "āَّm", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "mā"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "عَبَدتُّمْ", "translit": "ْmُّtdَbَع", "root": "عبد", "latinTranslit": "ʿabadttum"}], "hasIhya": false, "haleemTranslation": "I will never worship what you worship", "clearyTranslation": "I will never worship what you worship"}, {"ayah": 5, "text": "وَلَآ أَنتُمْ عَٰبِدُونَ مَآ أَعْبُدُ ", "translation": "Nor will you be worshippers of what I worship.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَلَآ", "translit": "ٓāَlَw", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "walā"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "أَنتُمْ", "translit": "ْmُtnَaʾ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "antum"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "عَٰبِدُونَ", "translit": "َnwُdِbَٰع", "root": "عبد", "latinTranslit": "ʿābidūna"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "مَآ", "translit": "ٓāَm", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "mā"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "أَعْبُدُ", "translit": "ُdُbْعَaʾ", "root": "عبد", "latinTranslit": "aʿbudu"}], "hasIhya": false, "haleemTranslation": "you will never worship what I worship", "clearyTranslation": "you will never worship what I worship"}, {"ayah": 6, "text": "لَكُمْ دِينُكُمْ وَلِىَ دِينِ ", "translation": "For you is your religion, and for me is my religion.\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "لَكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkَl", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "lakum"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "دِينُكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkُnyِd", "root": "دين", "latinTranslit": "dīnukum"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "وَلِىَ", "translit": "َàِlَw", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "waliya"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "دِينِ", "translit": "ِnyِd", "root": "دين", "latinTranslit": "dīni"}], "hasIhya": false, "haleemTranslation": "you have your religion and I have mine.’", "clearyTranslation": "you have your religion and I have mine.’"}], "tafsir": {"1": [{"arabic": "بأن يصلي ركعتين يقرأ في الأولى فاتحة الكتاب وقل يا أيها الكافرون وفي الثانية الفاتحة وقل هو الله أحد فإذا فرغ دعا وقال اللهم إني أستخيرك بعلمك وأستقدرك بقدرتك وأسألك من فضلك العظيم فإنك تقدر ولا أقدر وتعلم ولا أعلم وأنت علام الغيوب اللهم إن كنت تعلم أن هذا الأمر خير لي في ديني ودنياي وعاقبة أمري وعاجله وآجله فاقدره لي وبارك لي فيه ثم يسره لي وإن كنت تعلم أن هذا الأمر شر لي في ديني ودنياي وعاقبة أمري وعاجله وآجله فاصرفني عنه واصرفه عني واقدر لي الخير أينما كان إ", "english": "**The second degree:** That which is not frequently repeated but has significance — like the contract of marriage and the commencement of sincere counsel (al-naṣīḥah) and consultation (al-mushāwarah). The recommended practice therein is to begin with praise of God (al-ḥamd). Thus the one giving in marriage says: \"Praise be to God, and blessings upon the Messenger of God — may God bless him and grant him peace — I give you my daughter in marriage.\" And the one accepting says: \"Praise be to God, and blessings upon the Messenger of God — may God bless him and grant him peace — I accept the marriage.\" It was the custom of the Companions — may God be pleased with them — at the commencement of delivering a message, giving counsel, and consultation, to precede [these] with the praise of God (al-taḥmīd).", "book_title": "Ihya Ulum al-Din", "section_title_ar": "Section 164", "section_title_en": "Section 164", "section_index": 164, "anchors": "*(Anchors established)*", "content_type": "dua", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on supplications, prayers, and their spiritual power, from 'Ihya Ulum al-Din'", "badge": "Du'a"}, {"arabic": "وفي الصبح في السفر قل يا أيها الكافرون وقل هو الله أحد وكذلك في ركعتي الفجر والطواف والتحية وهو في جميع ذلك مستديم للقيام ووضع اليدين كما وصفنا في أول الصلاة", "english": "Then he recites a chapter (sūrah) or the equivalent of three verses of the Qurʾān or more. He does not connect the end of the chapter to the bowing takbīr (takbīr al-hawī) with a continuous junction; rather, he separates between them by the measure of saying \"Subḥān Allāh\" (Glory be to Allah). He recites in the dawn prayer from the longer chapters of the Muṣaṣṣal (the section of the Qurʾān from Qāf or al-Ḥujurāt to the end), and in the sunset prayer from its shorter ones. In the noon (Ẓuhr), afternoon (ʿAṣr), and night (ʿIshāʾ) prayers, he recites something similar to it — such as {«والسماء ذات البروج»} (By the heaven full of constellations) and what is near it in length.", "book_title": "Ihya Ulum al-Din", "section_title_ar": "Section 127", "section_title_en": "Section 127 — On the Recitation (Qirāʾah) and the Bowing (Rukūʿ) with Its Attendant Matters", "section_index": 127, "anchors": "- Root: قرأ (Qaraʾa)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of gathering and assembling letters into articulated speech; recitation as the oral articulation of written or memorized text.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): In the Quranic and liturgical context, qirāʾah is the deliberate, worshipful utterance of the divine word, distinct from mere reading; it is an act of devotion requiring presence of heart.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah) usage denotes the physical articulation of sounds; metaphorical (majāz) usage extends to the inward recitation of the heart in contemplation.\n- Root: ركع (Rakaʿa)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The physical act of bowing; the bending of the back in humility.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الركوع: الانحناء، فتارة يستعمل في الهيئة المخصوصة في الصلاة كما هي، وتارة في التواضع والتذلل\" — The bowing is an inclination, used sometimes for the specific posture in prayer, and sometimes for humility and self-abasement.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah): \"شيخ راكع: منحن من الكبر\" — an old man bowed with age. Metaphorical (majāz): \"لغبت الإبل حتى ركعت\" — camels fatigued until they lowered their heads, indicating the physical posture as the outward sign of inward submission.\n- Root: صبح (Ṣabaḥa)\n  * Al-Raghib (Al-Mufradat): \"الصبح والصباح، أول النهار\" — the dawn is the beginning of the day, the time when the horizon reddens.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas - Majaz/Metaphorical): \"أصبح يا رجل: انتبه من غفلتك\" — \"become awake, O man: awaken from your heedlessness,\" showing the dawn prayer as the time of spiritual awakening.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Rule of Prepositional Interposition (باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور) — governing the permissible separation between governing and governed elements, applied here to the proper pausing (waqf) and connecting (waṣl) of Quranic phrases to preserve meaning.", "content_type": "fiqh", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on the inner and outer dimensions of worship, from 'Ihya Ulum al-Din'", "badge": "Worship"}]}, "wordTiming": {"minshawi": {"1": [[1, 500, 946], [2, 946, 4228], [3, 4228, 5960]], "109:1": [[1, 500, 946], [2, 946, 4228], [3, 4228, 5960]], "2": [[1, 280, 2122], [2, 2122, 3023], [3, 3023, 3772], [4, 3772, 5320]], "109:2": [[1, 280, 2122], [2, 2122, 3023], [3, 3023, 3772], [4, 3772, 5320]], "3": [[1, 370, 2851], [2, 2851, 3757], [3, 3757, 5407], [4, 5407, 7370], [5, 7370, 8330]], "109:3": [[1, 370, 2851], [2, 2851, 3757], [3, 3757, 5407], [4, 5407, 7370], [5, 7370, 8330]], "4": [[1, 470, 2569], [2, 2569, 3464], [3, 3464, 4550], [4, 4550, 5445], [5, 5445, 6540]], "109:4": [[1, 470, 2569], [2, 2569, 3464], [3, 3464, 4550], [4, 4550, 5445], [5, 5445, 6540]], "5": [[1, 180, 2592], [2, 2592, 3473], [3, 3473, 5078], [4, 5078, 6987], [5, 6987, 7920]], "109:5": [[1, 180, 2592], [2, 2592, 3473], [3, 3473, 5078], [4, 5078, 6987], [5, 6987, 7920]], "6": [[1, 200, 865], [2, 865, 2285], [3, 2285, 3394], [4, 3394, 4370]], "109:6": [[1, 200, 865], [2, 865, 2285], [3, 2285, 3394], [4, 3394, 4370]]}, "abdulbasit": {"1": [[1, 950, 1150], [2, 1160, 3610], [3, 3620, 5220]], "109:1": [[1, 950, 1150], [2, 1160, 3610], [3, 3620, 5220]], "2": [[1, 500, 1630], [2, 1640, 2510], [3, 2520, 2850], [